# task0027_where_single_file_rejected.txtar — TASK-0027 / FR-21:
# --where on a plain single-file invocation (no -i, no STDIN) must be
# rejected at flag-parse time (PreRunE), BEFORE any file read, with exit 1
# and a canonical flag.conflict error on stderr.
#
# --where is a batch/stream filter; in single-file mode the predicate is
# silently ignored by runOnce. FR-21 requires that silent-ignore is replaced
# by a hard error so the user gets a clear diagnostic.
#
# CRITICAL: rejection MUST happen before any file read. We use a nonexistent
# file path and assert the error is flag.conflict, NOT an IO error. That
# proves the check fires at flag-parse time per DR-001.

# 1. Single non-existent file — must fail before any IO.
! nesdit does-not-exist.json --where '.type == "service"' --query '.'
stderr '^nesdit: error: flag\.conflict: '
! stderr 'does-not-exist'
! stderr 'io\.read'
! stdout .

# 2. Same rejection applies when the file exists — the error fires before open.
! nesdit f.json --where '.type == "service"' --query '.'
stderr '^nesdit: error: flag\.conflict: --where is a batch/stream filter'
! stdout .

# 3. Combining --where with -i (multi-file mode) IS valid — must succeed.
nesdit -i --create-missing f.json --where '.type == "service"' --query '.patched = true'
! stderr 'flag\.conflict'

-- f.json --
{"type":"service"}
